          "import { useCallback, useEffect, useEffectEvent, useRef } from \"react\";",
          "  // INITIAL LOAD - loadChats is a no-op once chats are loaded or loading\n  useEffect(() => {\n    loadChats();\n  }, [loadChats]);",
          "  // Auto-save when generation completes. The effect passes in the message\n  // count and last message id so that a change to either triggers a save,\n  // while edits to the rest of the chat (e.g. the prompt) do not.\n  const onMessagesSettled = useEffectEvent(\n    (_messageCount: number, _lastMessageId: string | undefined) => {\n      saveCurrentChat();\n    }\n  );\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!lastMessageInHistory?.isGenerating) {\n      onMessagesSettled(chatHistory.messages?.length, lastMessageInHistory?.id);\n    }\n  }, ["
        ],
        "finalMessage": "I fixed all four issues in `useTTDChatStorage.ts`, but I couldn't re-lint: there's no `node_modules` here, so the fixes are checked by reasoning, not by a lint run.\n\n**Issues 1 and 3 (initial load effect, line 187):** I added `loadChats` to the dependency list. This doesn't cause extra loads, because `loadChats` returns straight away once chats are loaded or loading (`chatsLoaded || isLoading`). Re-running it when its identity changes is therefore harmless. One small difference: if other code sets the exported `chatsLoadedAtom` back to `false` while the hook is mounted, chats will now reload. Before, they only loaded on mount.\n\n**Issues 2 and 4 (auto-save effect, line 254):** I left `saveCurrentChat` out of the dependency list. It depends on the whole `chatHistory`, so including it would save on every prompt keystroke and call `persistenceAdapter.saveChats` each time. Instead:\n- The save now runs through a `useEffectEvent` (`onMessagesSettled`), which always calls the latest `saveCurrentChat` without making it a dependency.\n- The effect passes the message count and last message id into that event as arguments. This is the pattern React's docs recommend. It means the effect really uses those two values, so the \"unnecessary dependency\" warnings go away. The event doesn't need the values, so its parameters start with `_`.\n- The dependency list is unchanged, so the effect still fires on exactly the same changes as before and saves only when the last message isn't generating.\n\nTwo risks I couldn't check without the linter:\n- The fix assumes oxlint's two hook rules treat the `useEffectEvent` function as not needing to be a dependency. Current versions of both rules do.\n- It assumes the `_` prefix keeps `no-unused-vars` quiet about the unused parameters. That's oxlint's default, but I couldn't see whether Ultracite's config overrides it.\n\n`useEffectEvent` is available in the React 19.3 this project lists in `package.json`.",
